\title{Method Locality: Factoring Algorithms Whose Cost Tracks the Factor, Not the Modulus}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give an exact structural account of a phenomenon long observed in computational
number theory: the running cost of Pollard's rho method and of the elliptic curve
method against a composite $N=pq$ is determined by the prime $p$ being hunted and is
independent of the cofactor $q$, while the cost of trial division is not. We separate
two properties conflated by the informal phrase ``factor-local'' --- \emph{cofactor
flatness} (the cost on $pq$ does not depend on $q$) and \emph{factor boundedness} (the
cost is bounded by a function of $p$ alone) --- and prove a shadow theorem (every state
update natural in the commutative ring has a mod-$p$ shadow equal to the mod-$p$ run,
hence is exactly cofactor flat and bounded by $p$); a rigidity theorem (a
modulus-determined, cofactor-flat cost model is constant on composites, so trial
division's non-flatness is forced); an exact elliptic-curve stage-1 ledger whose success
rate $\gcd(m,k(B))/m$ is cofactor-free, with curve count bounded by the Hasse ceiling of
the factor, antitone in the smoothness bound and equal to $1$ past the Hasse window; and
exact two-prime outcome counts locating the only genuine degeneracy wall at $\max(p,q)$,
together with a faithfulness analysis showing that a wall reported at $\min(p,q)$ is the
image of a single non-injective conflation in the accounting ledger.
\end{abstract}

